  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 232 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: The negatively tilted upper level trough will be in no \nhurry to exit the region today with the surface low pressure system \nexpected to slowly slide offshore around daybreak. We will remain \nunder the influence of cyclonic flow and plentiful deep layer \nmoisture with PWAT values remaining in the 1.3 to 1.6 inch range. \nCooler temperatures today will limit daytime heating and instability \nbut it's plausible that we see a few breaks throughout the day, \nespecially across eastern Vermont. These breaks could yield 500 J/kg \nof CAPE which would be sufficient to produce a few thunderstorms. \nThe main focus on heavier precipitation today will be across the \nConnecticut River Valley which is an area that was not hit hard over \nthe past 48 hours. While we remain in a marginal risk for flash \nflooding throughout the day, it's unlikely we will see the rainfall \nrates needed to yield flash flooding. Still, the slow motion of the \nstorms will be concerning and will bear watching this afternoon and \nevening. \n\nAfter days of rainfall, high pressure will settle into the region \nFriday and Saturday with drier weather expected during this \ntimeframe. Temperatures will warm closer to seasonal normals as we \nstart to see some sunshine while dewpoints will remain in the mid \n50s to lower 60s. It's likely that we see some patchy dense fog \nFriday and Saturday mornings given a subsidence inversion and \nsaturated soils from recent rainfall.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A slow moving upper level trough will move over New \nEngland on Sunday and is expected to take on a neutral so slightly \nnegative tilt. Both the ECMWF and GFS have this transition to a \nslower moving system Sunday afternoon into the early Monday \ntimeframe with the Canadian model showing a more southerly track and \nless phasing of the subtropical jet. There should be sufficient \nfrontal dynamics ahead of an approaching cold front coupled with \ndecent mid-level jet dynamics. In addition, deterministic guidance \nshows impressive PWAT values in the 1.7 to 1.9 inch range which is \nin the 90th + percentile. The slow moving front may yield another \nround of slow moving convection across the North Country and \nnorthern New York with rainfall totals in the 0.75 to 1.5 inch range \nvery much in play. Depending on the motion of the storms, WPC \nbelieves we could see some localized 3+ inches of rainfall and have \nplaced us in a marginal risk for flash flooding on Sunday. This \nsystem has some similarities to what we just saw across the Capital \nRegion of New York and is something to watch closely for the second \nhalf of the upcoming weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Cold front and attendant mid-level trough should be \nprogressing across the North Country from west to east Sunday night \nper consensus of 00Z NWP guidance suite. May see a few embedded \nthunderstorms with brief heavy rainfall with convective activity \nalong the front. However, the good news is that this system appears \nto be relatively progressive, with the highest PoPs before midnight \n(70-80% across VT) and then exiting eastward toward daybreak on \nMonday. Depending on translation speed of the front and overall \ntiming, would generally expect 0.5 to 1.0 inch rainfall most areas \nSunday night. A clearing, drying, and warming trend is expected \nthereafter for the Monday through Wednesday time frame. Valley highs \non Monday generally reach the lower 80s with dewpoints in the lower \n60s. Will see a gradual increase in temperatures and humidity levels \nthereafter with highs back in the mid 80s on Tuesday and in the \nupper 80s to near 90F for next Wednesday.",
